{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1451938983,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUxMjQ4MzExMjM=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/332","origin":"github"},"title":"How can I \"export\" a handle to give it to someone without git or git-annex?","message":"There are cases when data is only sent once, and it is easy to strip a handle down to the bare data instead of deploying git and git annex.\n#### rsync\n\nTransfer via SSH is best done with rsync:\n\n`rsync -rLv --safe-links --exclude=.git SRC DEST`\n\nwhere SRC is the to be transferred handle, and DEST is any destination URL that rsync can understand. This will only copy data that is actually present in the handle and will ignore all dead symlinks. It will also include annex-enabled submodules. This is useful if one manages data-dependency tracking via submodules. Again, only present files (i.e. actual dependency) are transferred. If a remote location is to be updated repeatedly, the `--delete` option may come handy.\n#### tar\n\nIf the handle needs to be exported into an archive tar does the job:\n\n`tar --exclude=\".git*\" -chvf DEST.tar SRC`\n\ninsert your favorite compression options as you see fit. Note that this command will return with a non-zero exit code if tar encounters dead symlinks. However, it will still complete the job and ignore them like rsync does.","files":null}]}